1
00:00:00,780 --> 00:00:06,680
In 1859, Bernhard Riemann wrote an
eight-page paper that tied the prime numbers

2
00:00:06,700 --> 00:00:10,680
to the zeros of a single
function, the zeta function.

3
00:00:10,700 --> 00:00:14,880
The zeros that matter lie in a
narrow strip of the complex plane,

4
00:00:14,900 --> 00:00:19,800
between zero and one. Riemann guessed
that all of them sit on one line,

5
00:00:19,820 --> 00:00:23,930
the line one half. That
guess is still open.

6
00:00:24,067 --> 00:00:28,847
Computers have since located more
than ten trillion of these zeros.

7
00:00:28,867 --> 00:00:32,657
Every one of them lies
exactly on that line.

8
00:00:34,060 --> 00:00:37,640
But checking zeros proves
nothing about all of them.

9
00:00:37,660 --> 00:00:42,280
What mathematicians can prove is more
modest: a region near the right edge of the

10
00:00:42,300 --> 00:00:44,840
strip where no zero can be.

11
00:00:44,860 --> 00:00:50,920
In 1896 that region was only the edge
itself, the line sigma equals one,

12
00:00:50,940 --> 00:00:54,090
and that was enough to prove
the prime number theorem.

13
00:00:54,200 --> 00:00:58,900
Three years later, de la Vallée
Poussin widened it into a sliver.

14
00:00:58,920 --> 00:01:01,460
The trouble is the shape of the sliver.

15
00:01:01,480 --> 00:01:06,420
As you climb to greater heights, it gets
thinner, and it thins toward nothing.

16
00:01:06,440 --> 00:01:11,060
The best version we have, from
Vinogradov and Korobov in 1958,

17
00:01:11,080 --> 00:01:12,470
still does.

18
00:01:13,927 --> 00:01:18,867
For a hundred and sixty-seven years, no
one could prove that any fixed vertical

19
00:01:18,887 --> 00:01:22,827
line, short of one, keeps every zero away.

20
00:01:22,847 --> 00:01:26,517
That statement is called a
quasi-Riemann hypothesis.

21
00:01:26,770 --> 00:01:32,693
In the autumn of 2026, OpenAI released
two manuscripts that claim to prove it.

22
00:01:32,713 --> 00:01:37,653
The first, dated September thirtieth,
claims that no zero lies to the right of

23
00:01:37,673 --> 00:01:43,093
seven eighths. The second, five days
later, isolates a simpler proof of a weaker

24
00:01:43,113 --> 00:01:48,693
line, eleven twelfths. Both lines hold
at every height, and for every Dirichlet

25
00:01:48,713 --> 00:01:51,303
L-function, not only zeta.

26
00:01:51,433 --> 00:01:54,293
This film explains how the arguments work.

27
00:01:54,313 --> 00:01:56,983
It does not certify that they are correct.

28
00:01:57,113 --> 00:02:01,303
That is the work of careful
readers, and it takes time.

29
00:02:07,827 --> 00:02:10,607
Here is the whole argument on one map.

30
00:02:10,627 --> 00:02:14,367
We will follow the eleven twelfths
proof, because it is the cleanest,

31
00:02:14,387 --> 00:02:17,407
and then see what the
seven eighths proof adds.

32
00:02:17,427 --> 00:02:20,737
There are four moves,
and an engine underneath.

33
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:27,310
Move one turns a question about zeros
into a question about cancellation.

34
00:02:27,520 --> 00:02:29,790
Take one over the L-function.

35
00:02:29,840 --> 00:02:34,340
Its series is built from Möbius
signs: plus one, minus one,

36
00:02:34,360 --> 00:02:40,780
or zero. If those signs cancel strongly
enough, one over L stays finite and smooth

37
00:02:40,800 --> 00:02:46,030
in a wider region. But a zero of
L would make one over L blow up.

38
00:02:46,160 --> 00:02:49,550
So enough cancellation means no zeros.

39
00:02:50,833 --> 00:02:54,543
Move two hides the target
sum inside a family.

40
00:02:54,753 --> 00:03:00,253
The proof moves to a number system with
six-fold symmetry and gives the sum a twist,

41
00:03:00,273 --> 00:03:03,423
a phase that depends
on a new parameter, u.

42
00:03:03,633 --> 00:03:06,863
Our sum is the member with u equal to one.

43
00:03:06,993 --> 00:03:12,133
The family as a whole obeys an energy budget:
add up the squares of all its members,

44
00:03:12,153 --> 00:03:15,583
and the total is about what
random signs would give.

45
00:03:16,867 --> 00:03:19,137
Move three makes copies.

46
00:03:19,267 --> 00:03:24,207
When u is the sixth power of a
prime, the twist almost disappears,

47
00:03:24,227 --> 00:03:27,777
and that member of the family
nearly equals the target.

48
00:03:27,907 --> 00:03:33,217
There are many such primes, so the target
appears many times inside the budget.

49
00:03:34,660 --> 00:03:36,800
Move four is arithmetic.

50
00:03:36,820 --> 00:03:41,560
If the target were large, every copy would
be large, and together they would overspend

51
00:03:41,580 --> 00:03:45,360
the budget. Count the copies,
divide the budget among them,

52
00:03:45,380 --> 00:03:49,920
and take a square root. The target can
be no bigger than about D to the eleven

53
00:03:49,940 --> 00:03:54,960
twelfths. Feed that into move one, and
no zero survives to the right of eleven

54
00:03:54,980 --> 00:03:56,130
twelfths.

55
00:03:57,400 --> 00:04:01,910
Everything rests on the energy
budget, and that is the hard part.

56
00:04:02,040 --> 00:04:07,620
It comes from an engine: Poisson summation,
which trades positions for frequencies;

57
00:04:07,640 --> 00:04:12,740
cubic Gauss sums; a theta function
from the theory of automorphic forms;

58
00:04:12,760 --> 00:04:17,620
a large sieve for quadratic characters;
and a recursion that shrinks the problem

59
00:04:17,640 --> 00:04:20,070
until it is small enough to count.

60
00:04:20,200 --> 00:04:24,180
The September manuscript reaches
seven eighths by a second route,

61
00:04:24,200 --> 00:04:26,660
which begins where this one ends.

62
00:04:26,680 --> 00:04:29,830
Now, the pieces, one at a time.

63
00:04:34,060 --> 00:04:38,280
First, why should zeros
know anything about primes?

64
00:04:38,300 --> 00:04:43,440
For sigma greater than one, zeta of
s is a sum over all whole numbers,

65
00:04:43,460 --> 00:04:46,280
and also a product over all primes.

66
00:04:46,300 --> 00:04:50,600
Expand the product and every
whole number appears exactly once,

67
00:04:50,620 --> 00:04:54,890
because every whole number factors
into primes in exactly one way.

68
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:58,790
That identity, Euler’s
product, is the bridge.

69
00:04:58,920 --> 00:05:02,620
The zeros of zeta control how
the primes are spread out,

70
00:05:02,640 --> 00:05:07,860
and how far the zeros keep from the line
sigma equals one controls how accurately

71
00:05:07,880 --> 00:05:09,350
we can count them.

72
00:05:10,633 --> 00:05:15,493
Dirichlet’s L-functions do the same
for primes in arithmetic progressions.

73
00:05:15,513 --> 00:05:20,773
A character, like this one modulo
four, weights each number by plus one,

74
00:05:20,793 --> 00:05:24,213
minus one, or zero
according to its remainder.

75
00:05:24,233 --> 00:05:28,693
The manuscripts claim their line for
every one of these functions at once,

76
00:05:28,713 --> 00:05:31,543
the same line whatever the modulus.

77
00:05:35,600 --> 00:05:40,750
The Möbius function, mu, supplies
the signs in one over zeta.

78
00:05:40,880 --> 00:05:45,100
It is zero when the number is
divisible by a square bigger than one.

79
00:05:45,120 --> 00:05:49,470
Otherwise it is minus one, raised
to the number of prime factors.

80
00:05:49,760 --> 00:05:55,150
Six gets plus one, thirty gets
minus one, and twelve gets zero.

81
00:05:56,433 --> 00:06:00,623
Add the signs up and you get
the Mertens function, M of x.

82
00:06:00,673 --> 00:06:03,693
Here it is, computed up to twelve hundred.

83
00:06:03,713 --> 00:06:09,263
Across twelve hundred terms, the total
never strays more than fifteen from zero.

84
00:06:09,400 --> 00:06:15,140
A bound like x to the alpha, with alpha
less than one, is what cancellation means

85
00:06:15,160 --> 00:06:18,630
in numbers. No picture can prove one.

86
00:06:20,060 --> 00:06:25,560
Here is the bridge. Partial summation
writes one over zeta as an integral of M

87
00:06:25,580 --> 00:06:28,080
of x against a power of x.

88
00:06:28,100 --> 00:06:33,400
If M of x grows no faster than x to the
alpha, the integral converges whenever

89
00:06:33,420 --> 00:06:38,000
sigma exceeds alpha, so one
over zeta is holomorphic there.

90
00:06:38,020 --> 00:06:40,010
It has no poles at all.

91
00:06:40,213 --> 00:06:44,433
A zero of zeta at rho would
be a pole of one over zeta.

92
00:06:44,453 --> 00:06:47,713
It cannot be both. That is the whole idea.

93
00:06:47,733 --> 00:06:51,043
The rest of the proof is about
earning the cancellation.

94
00:06:55,100 --> 00:06:57,930
The proof does not attack zeta directly.

95
00:06:57,980 --> 00:07:02,200
It moves to a richer number
system, the Eisenstein integers:

96
00:07:02,220 --> 00:07:08,120
numbers a plus b omega, where omega
is a complex cube root of one.

97
00:07:08,140 --> 00:07:11,640
They form a triangular lattice,
and they have six units,

98
00:07:11,660 --> 00:07:14,330
the six sixth roots of one.

99
00:07:14,467 --> 00:07:19,567
Every Dirichlet L-function reappears
here as a factor of an L-function of this

100
00:07:19,587 --> 00:07:25,377
field. So a line proved here comes back
down to zeta and all its relatives.

101
00:07:26,633 --> 00:07:32,693
In this world, the target is a smooth
sum, A one of D: Möbius values,

102
00:07:32,713 --> 00:07:37,463
weighted by a fixed character
nu, over ideals of norm about D.

103
00:07:37,593 --> 00:07:43,303
It has about D terms. The goal is to
show that it is much smaller than D.

104
00:07:44,727 --> 00:07:50,067
Now the twist. Six-fold symmetry
brings sextic residue symbols:

105
00:07:50,087 --> 00:07:55,827
for each n, a phase, chi n of u,
that is always a sixth root of one,

106
00:07:55,847 --> 00:07:59,907
or zero. Insert it, and the
single sum becomes a family,

107
00:07:59,927 --> 00:08:02,867
A u, one member for each u.

108
00:08:02,887 --> 00:08:06,347
Set u equal to one and every phase is one.

109
00:08:06,367 --> 00:08:08,437
That member is our target.

110
00:08:12,500 --> 00:08:16,690
The central estimate of the October
paper is about the whole family.

111
00:08:16,820 --> 00:08:22,080
Add up the squared sizes of A u,
for every u with norm up to H.

112
00:08:22,100 --> 00:08:27,840
The total is at most about D times
H, where H is a little bigger than D:

113
00:08:27,860 --> 00:08:32,050
D to the one plus theta, with
theta small and positive.

114
00:08:32,167 --> 00:08:37,267
There are about H members, so a typical
member has size about the square root of

115
00:08:37,287 --> 00:08:41,957
D. That is square-root
cancellation, on average.

116
00:08:43,313 --> 00:08:47,133
But an average says nothing
about any single member.

117
00:08:47,153 --> 00:08:52,253
One huge sum can hide among many
small ones, and the budget allows it.

118
00:08:52,273 --> 00:08:56,813
The member we care about, u
equal to one, is a single row.

119
00:08:56,833 --> 00:09:00,863
To pin it down, we need the
target to show up many times.

120
00:09:04,933 --> 00:09:11,283
Here is how. Take a prime p of the
lattice, and set u equal to p to the sixth.

121
00:09:11,333 --> 00:09:15,313
A sixth-order phase raised
to the sixth power is one.

122
00:09:15,333 --> 00:09:19,873
So the phase is one for every
n, except the n that p divides,

123
00:09:19,893 --> 00:09:21,443
where it is zero.

124
00:09:21,567 --> 00:09:26,557
The sixth-power row is the target,
minus the terms divisible by p.

125
00:09:26,687 --> 00:09:32,317
If p has norm about Y, there are
only about D over Y of those.

126
00:09:33,600 --> 00:09:35,310
How many copies are there?

127
00:09:35,520 --> 00:09:40,860
One for each prime with norm up
to Y: about Y over log Y of them,

128
00:09:40,880 --> 00:09:42,780
by the prime ideal theorem.

129
00:09:42,800 --> 00:09:47,820
Their sixth powers have norm up to Y to
the sixth, and to stay inside the family

130
00:09:47,840 --> 00:09:52,910
that must be at most H. So
Y is H to the one sixth.

131
00:09:54,167 --> 00:10:00,707
Now spend the budget. Each copy is the
target, up to an error of about D over Y.

132
00:10:00,727 --> 00:10:06,067
So the copies together cost about Y
over log Y times the target squared,

133
00:10:06,087 --> 00:10:09,077
and that cannot exceed D times H.

134
00:10:09,207 --> 00:10:13,907
Divide, and the target squared
is at most D times H over Y,

135
00:10:13,927 --> 00:10:15,717
plus the error squared.

136
00:10:17,080 --> 00:10:22,980
Put in Y equals H to the one sixth,
and H equals D to the one plus theta.

137
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:29,220
D times H to the five sixths is D to the
eleven sixths, plus five theta over six.

138
00:10:29,240 --> 00:10:33,860
Take the square root... eleven
twelfths, plus five theta over twelve.

139
00:10:33,880 --> 00:10:38,470
The error term gives only about D to
the five sixths, which is smaller.

140
00:10:38,600 --> 00:10:42,700
Theta can be as small as we like,
but it has to stay positive,

141
00:10:42,720 --> 00:10:44,500
because the engine needs it.

142
00:10:44,520 --> 00:10:49,180
So the target is at most D to
the eleven twelfths plus epsilon,

143
00:10:49,200 --> 00:10:52,700
for every epsilon. That
is the cancellation.

144
00:10:52,720 --> 00:10:55,750
Now we turn it into a
statement about zeros.

145
00:10:59,800 --> 00:11:04,340
The sums are smooth, so the right
tool is the Mellin transform:

146
00:11:04,360 --> 00:11:08,660
integrate A one of D
against D to the minus s.

147
00:11:08,680 --> 00:11:13,380
The bound we just proved makes this
integral converge for every s with real part

148
00:11:13,400 --> 00:11:17,910
above eleven twelfths, so the
transform is holomorphic there.

149
00:11:18,033 --> 00:11:22,493
Where the real part exceeds one,
you can compute it term by term.

150
00:11:22,513 --> 00:11:26,893
The answer is the Mellin transform
of the window, W hat of s,

151
00:11:26,913 --> 00:11:28,863
divided by the L-function.

152
00:11:28,993 --> 00:11:34,893
Multiply through: L times the transform
equals W hat, and that identity carries

153
00:11:34,913 --> 00:11:38,623
over to the whole half-plane
beyond eleven twelfths.

154
00:11:40,060 --> 00:11:45,400
Now suppose L had a zero,
rho, beyond eleven twelfths.

155
00:11:45,420 --> 00:11:50,640
The proof aims the window at it:
W of y is y to the minus rho,

156
00:11:50,660 --> 00:11:54,600
times a smooth bump, phi,
that is never negative.

157
00:11:54,620 --> 00:12:01,050
Then W hat at rho is the integral of
phi of y over y, a positive number.

158
00:12:01,373 --> 00:12:06,427
At rho, the left side is zero times
something finite, which is zero.

159
00:12:06,447 --> 00:12:08,507
The right side is positive.

160
00:12:08,527 --> 00:12:10,827
Zero equals a positive number!

161
00:12:10,847 --> 00:12:13,277
So no such zero exists.

162
00:12:17,367 --> 00:12:20,387
Everything so far assumed
the energy budget.

163
00:12:20,407 --> 00:12:22,597
Now for the engine that proves it.

164
00:12:22,727 --> 00:12:27,397
Expand the squares and apply Poisson
summation in the row variable u.

165
00:12:27,447 --> 00:12:32,437
Poisson summation trades a sum over
positions for a sum over frequencies.

166
00:12:32,567 --> 00:12:36,867
A long sum over u becomes a
short sum over a new variable,

167
00:12:36,887 --> 00:12:42,517
h, with about D squared over H
terms: D to the one minus theta.

168
00:12:43,800 --> 00:12:47,860
The Fourier transforms of the
sextic phases are Gauss sums,

169
00:12:47,880 --> 00:12:50,070
and here the arithmetic helps.

170
00:12:50,200 --> 00:12:54,710
For ordinary integers, the square
of a Gauss sum is just a sign.

171
00:12:54,840 --> 00:12:59,740
The paper uses a sextic relative of that
identity, which goes back to Hasse and

172
00:12:59,760 --> 00:13:01,110
Heath-Brown.

173
00:13:01,233 --> 00:13:06,493
It lets the Möbius values absorb a Gauss
sum, and what is left are cubic Gauss

174
00:13:06,513 --> 00:13:11,853
sums. The problem becomes the
energy of new sums, B h of X,

175
00:13:11,873 --> 00:13:16,383
built from cubic Gauss sums
and twisted by phases in h.

176
00:13:20,467 --> 00:13:23,217
Cubic Gauss sums are not random.

177
00:13:23,267 --> 00:13:27,967
Kubota and Patterson showed that they are
the Fourier coefficients of a cubic theta

178
00:13:27,987 --> 00:13:32,817
function, an automorphic form on
three-dimensional hyperbolic space.

179
00:13:33,027 --> 00:13:38,017
Automorphic forms have symmetry, and
symmetry gives a transformation law.

180
00:13:38,133 --> 00:13:41,123
To use it, the paper completes the sum.

181
00:13:41,173 --> 00:13:46,593
Alongside each square-free index
n, it includes n times b cubed,

182
00:13:46,613 --> 00:13:51,393
for every b, which are exactly the
terms the theta function contains.

183
00:13:51,413 --> 00:13:57,203
The transformation then turns a long
sum over n into a short dual sum.

184
00:13:58,600 --> 00:14:00,737
And the phases change character.

185
00:14:00,787 --> 00:14:06,167
At each prime dividing h, the Fourier
side contributes chi to the minus one,

186
00:14:06,187 --> 00:14:09,457
and the theta side contributes
chi to the minus two.

187
00:14:09,587 --> 00:14:14,087
Together that is chi to the minus
three, which equals chi cubed.

188
00:14:14,107 --> 00:14:17,927
And the cube of a sixth-order
phase is just plus or minus one.

189
00:14:17,947 --> 00:14:19,457
It is quadratic.

190
00:14:20,733 --> 00:14:25,753
That matters, because quadratic
characters have the sharpest large sieve.

191
00:14:25,773 --> 00:14:31,353
For a family with M rows and L columns,
it bounds the energy by M plus L,

192
00:14:31,373 --> 00:14:36,633
up to tiny losses. The general bound for
higher-order characters carries an extra

193
00:14:36,653 --> 00:14:41,113
term, M times L to the two thirds,
which would break the count.

194
00:14:41,133 --> 00:14:45,403
With the quadratic sieve, the
completed sums come in on budget.

195
00:14:49,467 --> 00:14:51,897
But completion borrowed something.

196
00:14:51,947 --> 00:14:54,937
The cube terms were never
in the original sum.

197
00:14:55,067 --> 00:14:59,977
Möbius inversion takes them back out,
and for small b that is harmless.

198
00:15:00,027 --> 00:15:04,607
For large b, it leaves remainders:
energies of the same kind,

199
00:15:04,627 --> 00:15:10,247
at a smaller column scale, X over the
norm of b cubed, but still over the full

200
00:15:10,267 --> 00:15:13,977
range of rows. That is not progress yet.

201
00:15:15,233 --> 00:15:20,333
So the paper applies Poisson summation
twice more, after deliberately widening

202
00:15:20,353 --> 00:15:25,263
an intermediate sum, which is allowed
because every term in it is non-negative.

203
00:15:25,393 --> 00:15:30,173
The result is the same kind of estimate,
with both scales divided by the norm of

204
00:15:30,193 --> 00:15:35,983
b cubed. Rows and columns shrink
together, and their ratio never changes.

205
00:15:36,180 --> 00:15:38,160
And the shrinking is real.

206
00:15:38,180 --> 00:15:43,170
Each step shrinks the row range by a
factor of at least D to the two theta.

207
00:15:43,300 --> 00:15:47,920
Because theta is positive, a bounded
number of steps brings every branch down to

208
00:15:47,940 --> 00:15:50,640
a size where simple counting is enough.

209
00:15:50,660 --> 00:15:54,450
That is why theta could
be small, but never zero.

210
00:15:54,567 --> 00:15:59,427
The full proof carries extra twists, common
factors, and changing weights through

211
00:15:59,447 --> 00:16:03,877
every step. But this is its
skeleton, and it closes.

212
00:16:03,927 --> 00:16:08,117
The energy budget is proved,
and eleven twelfths follows.

213
00:16:12,200 --> 00:16:16,740
The September manuscript goes further,
with a different engine built around one

214
00:16:16,760 --> 00:16:21,860
principle. Let beta star be the
largest real part of any zero,

215
00:16:21,880 --> 00:16:26,100
across all the Hecke L-functions
of this field, or the supremum,

216
00:16:26,120 --> 00:16:31,300
if no zero attains it. Suppose beta
star is bigger than a proposed line,

217
00:16:31,320 --> 00:16:32,630
sigma zero.

218
00:16:32,767 --> 00:16:37,707
For each target, the paper builds a
sum that it can bound in two ways.

219
00:16:37,727 --> 00:16:42,227
Reflection, from the cubic theta
transformation, bounds it directly.

220
00:16:42,247 --> 00:16:47,907
Poisson summation compares it with a signal
that contains one over the target L-function.

221
00:16:47,927 --> 00:16:52,107
If both bounds hold, with margins
that do not depend on the target,

222
00:16:52,127 --> 00:16:56,717
the reciprocal continues a fixed
distance to the left of beta star.

223
00:16:56,993 --> 00:17:01,653
But by the definition of a supremum,
some zero lies in that strip,

224
00:17:01,673 --> 00:17:04,293
and its reciprocal
would have a pole there.

225
00:17:04,313 --> 00:17:08,863
Contradiction. So beta
star is at most sigma zero.

226
00:17:10,133 --> 00:17:14,193
Part one of the paper runs this
machine at eleven twelfths.

227
00:17:14,213 --> 00:17:19,393
Part two starts from that result, so it
may assume that no zero lies beyond eleven

228
00:17:19,413 --> 00:17:21,713
twelfths, and it pushes further.

229
00:17:21,733 --> 00:17:26,833
It rebuilds the sum with compensating
prime factors, uses two unequal averaging

230
00:17:26,853 --> 00:17:31,553
scales, and adds moment bounds for
two kinds of Dirichlet polynomials,

231
00:17:31,573 --> 00:17:34,163
each proved by its own recursion.

232
00:17:34,437 --> 00:17:40,560
The signal’s exponent moves from s minus
two thirds to s minus eleven sixteenths,

233
00:17:40,580 --> 00:17:43,240
and the margins close at seven eighths.

234
00:17:43,260 --> 00:17:48,570
The stronger result is a bootstrap: the
first line is the foothold for the second.

235
00:17:52,633 --> 00:17:58,373
Why does a theorem about this six-fold world
settle questions about ordinary primes?

236
00:17:58,393 --> 00:18:03,303
Lift a Dirichlet character, chi, to
the Eisenstein field through the norm.

237
00:18:03,433 --> 00:18:09,253
Its L-function splits into two ordinary
ones: L of chi, and L of chi times the

238
00:18:09,273 --> 00:18:15,143
character modulo three. A zero of either
factor would be a zero of the product.

239
00:18:15,273 --> 00:18:20,903
So the line transfers to every Dirichlet
L-function, including zeta itself.

240
00:18:22,167 --> 00:18:25,747
Zeros control prime counts
through the explicit formula,

241
00:18:25,767 --> 00:18:31,717
where a zero, rho, contributes a wave
of size about x to the real part of rho.

242
00:18:31,927 --> 00:18:36,627
Keep every real part at most eleven
twelfths, and the error in counting primes

243
00:18:36,647 --> 00:18:42,547
up to x, in any progression, is at most a
constant times x to the eleven twelfths,

244
00:18:42,567 --> 00:18:44,117
times log x.

245
00:18:44,233 --> 00:18:50,133
That holds for every modulus up to x, and
the constant is absolute and effective:

246
00:18:50,153 --> 00:18:53,013
it could, in principle, be computed.

247
00:18:53,033 --> 00:18:57,173
For large moduli, that error can
be bigger than the count itself,

248
00:18:57,193 --> 00:19:00,053
so this is not the last
word on progressions.

249
00:19:00,073 --> 00:19:05,013
It also rules out Landau–Siegel zeros,
the exceptional zeros that have shadowed

250
00:19:05,033 --> 00:19:07,703
the subject for nearly a century.

251
00:19:11,927 --> 00:19:14,627
Some consequences are easy to state.

252
00:19:14,647 --> 00:19:20,787
Pick an odd prime, p. Half of the nonzero
numbers modulo p are perfect squares,

253
00:19:20,807 --> 00:19:25,557
and half are not. How far must you
search to find one that is not?

254
00:19:25,667 --> 00:19:31,567
Usually, not far. Among all the primes
below a million, the worst case is first

255
00:19:31,587 --> 00:19:38,687
met at 366,791, where the
first non-square is 43.

256
00:19:38,707 --> 00:19:43,377
Vinogradov conjectured that you never
need to go beyond p to the epsilon.

257
00:19:43,507 --> 00:19:48,767
The September manuscript states that a
fixed zero-free line gives much more:

258
00:19:48,787 --> 00:19:53,377
the first non-square is at
most a fixed power of log p.

259
00:19:54,667 --> 00:20:00,487
That makes the search fast, so square roots
modulo a prime can be found by a deterministic

260
00:20:00,507 --> 00:20:04,647
algorithm in polynomial
time, with no coin flips.

261
00:20:04,667 --> 00:20:08,967
Miller’s primality test becomes
deterministic and unconditional.

262
00:20:08,987 --> 00:20:14,247
And Euler’s list of sixty-five idoneal
numbers, an open question since the eighteenth

263
00:20:14,267 --> 00:20:17,497
century, would be confirmed complete.

264
00:20:18,927 --> 00:20:23,707
Step back to the map. A century
and a half of proven regions,

265
00:20:23,727 --> 00:20:26,507
every one thinning toward the edge.

266
00:20:26,527 --> 00:20:31,147
If these manuscripts are right, there
is now a line that does not move:

267
00:20:31,167 --> 00:20:35,037
seven eighths, at every
height, for every character.

268
00:20:35,167 --> 00:20:38,477
And the line one half is still far away.

269
00:20:38,607 --> 00:20:43,467
Between seven eighths and one half lies
the rest of the Riemann hypothesis,

270
00:20:43,487 --> 00:20:47,227
and much of what we believe about
the primes but cannot prove.

271
00:20:47,247 --> 00:20:49,757
The manuscripts say so themselves.

272
00:20:51,113 --> 00:20:55,253
Results like this are checked the
way mathematics always checks them:

273
00:20:55,273 --> 00:20:58,933
line by line, by people who read slowly.

274
00:20:58,953 --> 00:21:03,973
If this line holds, it is one of the great
advances in the history of the subject.

275
00:21:03,993 --> 00:21:09,653
A border that holds, after a hundred and
sixty-seven years of borders that shrank.

276
00:21:09,673 --> 00:21:13,223
And beyond it, the ground
is still unsurveyed.
